{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And Jacob boiled a stew.</b> It should have said 'and Isaac boiled a stew,' since he was mourning for Abraham. But because a mourner is forbidden to eat of his own food, therefore Jacob prepared this dish. It was a lentil stew, and the custom is to eat it in the house of mourning, because a lentil is closed and round like a wheel, to say: the wheel turns in the world. And just as this lentil has no mouth, so the mourner is forbidden to speak. On that day Abraham departed to his eternal home, and it was the merit of Abraham that he did not see Esau going out to a bad culture. And so the Rabbis, blessed be their memory, expounded: five transgressions did that wicked man commit on that day: he lay with a betrothed maiden, and killed a soul, and denied the resurrection of the dead, and denied the Root, and despised the birthright. He lay with a betrothed maiden — it is written here: ״ויבא עשו מן השדה״, and it is written there (Deuteronomy 22:27): ״כי בשדה מצאה״. He killed a soul — it is written here: ״והוא עיף״, and it is written there (Jeremiah 4:31): ״כי עיפה נפשי להורגים״. He denied the resurrection of the dead — it is written here: ״הנה אנכי הולך״, and it is written there (Job 7:9): ״כלה ענן וילך״. He denied the Root — it is written here: ״למה זה לי״, and it is written there (Exodus 15:2): ״זה אלי ואנוהו״. He despised the birthright — as it is said: ״ויבז עשו את הבכורה״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:08.705395635Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}